# Gotchas

Only what isn't visible from the imports and breaks silently. Don't rewrite or
delete entries, only append new ones.

- **`cleanreport.CLEANABLE` is the real cleaning gate in the bot.** Adding a
  format to `core.clean._CLEANERS` isn't enough: `bot._offer_cleaning` checks
  `CLEANABLE` specifically and without it silently won't show the buttons, so
  the bot will refuse to clean a format the core already cleans.
- **`bot._EXT` and `bot._STEM` have to be extended together with `CLEANABLE`.**
  Otherwise the cleaned file goes to the user named `image_ab12cd.bin`.
- **Two tables of phase numbers:** `core.clean._PLANNED` and
  `cleanreport._PHASE`. `cleanreport._no_cleaner()` prefers the dispatcher's
  `planned_phase`, its own table is the fallback, so a mismatch between them
  doesn't fail the tests but waits quietly. For RTF/ODF they already differ
  (2 vs 4).
- **ZIP timestamps in the fixtures mean different things.**
  `fixtures.make_docx` is built by a library and carries real time,
  `make_docx_wordlike` has `1980-01-01`, like Word. So "timestamps kept as in
  the original" is a correct requirement only for the second one: for the
  first, keeping them means leaving the save time leak, and `clean_ooxml`
  deliberately brings them to `1980-01-01`.
- **OOXML part XML is edited byte by byte, not through lxml/ElementTree.** A
  round trip through a serializer reorders `xmlns`, turns `\r\n` into `\n` and
  writes `<a/>`: a processing fingerprint on every changed part.
- **`STEALTH` for OOXML is clean but not undetectable.** Empty `dc:creator` +
  `cp:lastModifiedBy` are themselves a `scrubbed` signal for the inspector. A
  limit of the format, not a defect; the dispatcher passes it on in
  `DispatchResult.new_signals`, and the report has to warn the user.
- **PowerShell 5.1 on this machine doesn't understand `&&`.** Separate commands
  with `;`, and check `$LASTEXITCODE` right after the run, not after a pipe.
- **`bot._no_clean_text()` takes the phase number from `cleanreport._PHASE`,
  not from the dispatcher:** it builds an empty `CleanResult` whose
  `planned_phase=0`. So the bot's message BEFORE cleaning and the report AFTER
  come from different tables, and the user will see a mismatch. The RTF/ODF
  mismatch was resolved in favor of phase 2.
- **A "the part is gone" check is easily vacuous.** `printerSettings*.bin`
  exists only in `fixtures.make_pptx`, `word/people.xml` only in `make_docx`.
  Requiring their absence on a fixture that never had them checks nothing;
  compare against `src_order`, like `test_20_ooxml_cleaning` does.
- **Raw XML as a finding's value poisons the cleaning oracle.** The oracle
  looks for the original value in the output; a dump of a part contains tag
  names and schema URIs that are the same in every document of that type, so a
  legitimately kept part counts as a "surviving value" and a correctly cleaned
  file gets a "not fully cleaned" verdict. Return only values: text plus
  attributes.
- **A verbal placeholder instead of an empty value is a "surviving value" too.**
  The string "no values" in an `ENVIRONMENT` finding matches itself. If there
  are no values, it isn't a leak: use the `PROVENANCE` risk, which the oracle
  doesn't check.
- **The `customXml` root lists are duplicated in two modules and must match:**
  `insp_ooxml._CX_SHAREPOINT_NS` / `_CX_CONTENT_NS` and
  `clean_ooxml._SP_ROOT_NS` / `_CONTENT_ROOT_NS`. The inspector must not depend
  on the cleaner, so the match is guarded by
  `tests.test_clean.test_15_customxml_namespaces_agree`.
- **`insp_ooxml._sec_excel` is called for every format, not only Excel.** It
  holds the `customXml/` parse, which real `.docx` files have more often than
  `.xlsx`. The section name is misleading.
- **`<tg-spoiler>` wraps `<code>`, not the other way round.** Telegram forbids
  nested entities inside `code` and `pre` but allows them inside `spoiler`. The
  reverse order produces invalid HTML and Telegram rejects the whole message.
- **Value masking lives in one flag, `core.report.MASK_VALUES`**, and
  `core/cleanreport.py` imports `spoiler()` from there. The cleaning report
  lists the same values as the inspection report: adding a place that prints a
  value and forgetting the spoiler means leaking into the Telegram chat history.
- **`PARANOID` on a file from Word gives an `ai` signal, not just `scrubbed`.**
  The profile removes rsids, and the inspector concludes the package was built
  by a library. The file looks not cleaned but machine-generated, which is
  worse for many purposes. Measured with `python -m core.clean_ooxml --real-word`.
- **Acceptance testing on real files is `tests/real_office.py`.** Office is
  optional: no Word means `Unavailable`, and the caller has to survive that.
  Three tricks inside are non-obvious and each cost a debugging session:
  `InvokeMember` instead of direct access to COM properties (otherwise `$null`
  and an invisible modal "Save As" dialog), turning off
  `Options.WarnBeforeSavingPrintingSendingMarkup` (otherwise a hang on a file
  with tracked changes) and UTF-8 **with a BOM** for `.ps1` (PowerShell 5.1
  reads a script without a BOM as cp1251).
- **ODF ZIP timestamps depend on the producer, not on the format.** Word saving
  to `.odt` writes `1980-01-01`; LibreOffice writes the real time. `clean_odf`
  has to decide by `meta:generator`, otherwise it leaves a fingerprint on half
  the files. The old wording in ROADMAP ("ODF has real timestamps") was a wrong
  generalization.
- **Dates in `core.xml` are accurate to the minute, Word's seconds are always
  `00`.** So `created == modified` is an ordinary state, not a sign of forgery,
  and `cp:revision` is no corroboration (Word 16 had `revision=2` after four
  saves within one minute). Only `TotalTime` gives evidence.
- **`\*\revtbl` in RTF starts with the service entry `Unknown`.** Word always
  writes it. Taking it for a person means an extra `IDENTITY` finding, an
  inflated author count and, most importantly, a poisoned cleaning oracle: the
  word is longer than `clean.MIN_VALUE_LEN` and "survives" any cleaning.
- **Check RTF paths after decoding, not before.** `_PATH_RE` works on the raw
  text, where backslashes are doubled and control words sit between
  characters; `\\\hich\f37` looks like UNC but decodes into a single `\`. Hence
  the second filter, `_PATH_DECODED_RE`.
- **`SttbfRMark` in `.doc` starts with `Unknown`, like `\*\revtbl` in RTF.**
  The `fc`/`lcb` pair is index 51 in `FibRgFcLcb`; the base is computed from
  `csw`/`cslw` in the file, not a constant 154. The service entry must not be
  taken for a person.
- **The renderer doesn't limit the number of messages, `bot._MAX_PARTS` does.**
  Splitting goes by the 4096-character limit, and hundreds of findings make
  dozens of messages; Telegram answers 429 and temporarily bans the bot. The
  limit has to sit in the handler: only it knows the recipient is a chat, not a
  console.
- **pikepdf: `dct.get(key)` crashes on a non-UTF-8 key name, `dct[key]`
  doesn't.** A name like `/Auth#9Dor` comes out of `keys()` as a string with a
  surrogate, and `get()` raises `TypeError`; `[]`, `in` and `del` with the same
  string work. When iterating over the keys of a PDF dictionary, read values
  only through `[]` (or `insp_pdf._get`).
- **lxml won't take Latin-1 XMP declared as UTF-8, even with `recover=True`.**
  Parse only through `insp_pdf._xmp_fromstring` (it fixes the bytes and
  retries). And in tests: `pdf.save()` by default (`fix_metadata_version=True`)
  itself replaces such XMP with an empty one, so the fixture silently stops
  being broken.
- **Defanging links before LibreOffice compares the DECODED value.**
  `&#104;ttp://` in the raw bytes doesn't look like http, but LO's XML parser
  reads it as an address and goes to the network. Via `file://` LO opens the
  file (measured by access time), and `file://server/...` on Windows means SMB
  and the owner's NTLM hash.
- **`model._ABS_PATH` only catches a path at the start of a "word".** Without
  that, the part name `word/media/image1.png` in an error text turned into
  `word<path hidden>`, and `http://` into `htt<path hidden>`. Add new prefixes
  with the same caveat.
- **`meta:template` in ODF is empty: the path is in `xlink:href`.** Reading
  `el.text` on ODF link elements is useless; the same goes for
  `meta:auto-reload`.
- **Only presentation changes with the language, never a verdict.** `tr()`
  reads the language from a ContextVar at call time, so calling it at import
  time freezes one language: module-level display constants are marked with
  `N_()` and wrapped in `tr()` where they're shown. Logic must never compare a
  translated string with an untranslated one; the oracle's presence markers
  (present/absent, yes/no, empty, not set, on/off) are matched in both
  languages through `both()` (`clean._PLACEHOLDERS`). A new `tr()` string
  without a Russian entry in `core/i18n_ru.py` fails `tests/test_i18n.py`,
  which also checks that every fixture gets identical verdicts in both
  languages.
- **Outside a handler everything speaks English.** The bot wraps each update in
  `use_lang()` (`_LangMiddleware`); the CLI, the tests and the console messages
  at startup run with the default language, English. Test substring checks
  match the English text for that reason.
